  "result": "All five tests pass.\n\nI implemented `ratelimit/limiter.py` with **Approach B, the sliding window log**. Each key holds a deque of allowed-call timestamps. On every call, entries at or before `now - window_seconds` are evicted from the front, and the call is allowed only if fewer than `limit` entries remain.\n\nI chose the sliding window log because it gives exact per-window accounting with only integer comparisons on caller-supplied floats. A token bucket would compute refill as `limit / window_seconds * elapsed`, which can land a hair under a whole token at exact window boundaries and fail the \"full burst restores at exactly one window\" case.\n\nOne judgment call worth noting: the spec says to drop timestamps \"older than\" the cutoff, but the tests require that a call made exactly one window ago no longer counts. I treated the boundary as inclusive for eviction so a full burst recurs at exactly `now == window_seconds`. The constructor also rejects a limit below one or a non-positive window.",
